<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spahis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spahis were light cavalry regiments of the French Army recruited mainly among the Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> populations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, first organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1834. Their name came from the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">sipahi</a> cavalry. Spahi units served in French colonial campaigns, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> and in both world wars, and were also raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spahis.jpg" alt="Spahis" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1st_Spahis_standard_guard_Bastille_Day_2008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1830, irregular horsemen recruited locally were attached to the light cavalry regiments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> as chasseurs spahis. Between 1834 and 1836 they became four squadrons of regular spahis, in 1841 the fourteen squadrons then in existence were joined in a single corps, and in 1845 three regiments were formed, the 1st of Algiers, the 2nd of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> and the 3rd of Constantine. The name is the French form of the Ottoman Turkish sipahi, from the Persian word for soldier or horseman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The troopers were mostly Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a>, about a fifth French, under French officers, with a fixed number of commissions up to captain reserved for Muslim officers. Unlike the North African infantry, the spahis came from the higher social classes, a rule dating from Colonel Marey-Monge's requirement that each recruit bring his own horse. They usually served in small detachments as scouts, skirmishers and escorts, though at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-isly">Battle of Isly</a> in 1844 two regiments fought as full units. Their Zouave style dress of a red jacket, sash, light blue trousers and white burnous made them conspicuous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The regiments served in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a> campaign of 1860, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> and in Tonkin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. A mutiny of a squadron of the 3rd Spahis ordered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sparked the Algerian uprising of 1871–72. By 1918 seven regiments had served on the Western Front, and by 1921 there were twelve. Still mounted in 1940, three regiments fought in France, and a Moroccan regiment that crossed into Transjordan joined the Free French, was mechanised by the British in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and helped liberate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in August 1944.</p><p>Armoured spahi units fought in the Indochina War and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a>. After 1962 all but one regiment had been disbanded; the 1er Régiment de Spahis, formerly Moroccan, remains an armoured unit of the French Army, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a>. Senegal's Red Guard and mounted units of the Algerian Republican Guard and the Tunisian President's Bodyguard keep spahi traditions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spahi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French Foreign Legion was founded in 1831 for service there and later made its headquarters at Sidi Bel Abbès, and the colony also produced the Zouaves, the Tirailleurs algériens, the <b>Spahis</b> and the Chasseurs d’Afrique.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li></ul></section>
